    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented and why it keeps structural impact low.\n\n## Changes\n\n**1. `openapi.yml`** (request/response contract)\n- Added optional `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` properties to the `OwnerFields` schema (same normalization-friendly constraints as `address`).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list, so a structured owner (no flat `address`) is accepted while `city` and the rest stay required. The flat `address` remains an accepted input for backward compatibility.\n\n**2. `Owner.java`** (domain behaviour)\n- Added three `@Transient` holders: `flatAddress`, `addressLine1`, `addressLine2`.\n- Added trivial `getAddressLine1/2` + `setAddressLine1/2` accessors that normalize on input.\n- Reworked `setAddress` to normalize into `flatAddress`, then recompute the persisted `address`.\n- Added one small private `composeAddress()` that prefers the structured lines (`addressLine1`, plus `addressLine2` after a single space when present) and falls back to the flat address.\n\n## Why this is low-impact\n\n- **Two files touched**, and no third-party wiring: MapStruct maps `addressLine1/2` by name automatically, so `OwnerMapper`, the controllers, and validators were left untouched.\n- Every reader of the address goes through the existing `getAddress()` (which returns the composed value), so the household hash, postcode validation and locality transparently use the structured fields when present and fall back to the flat address \u2014 no edits to those large call sites.\n- Each new/changed method in `Owner` is a small, single-purpose unit (getters/setters CC=1, `composeAddress` CC=2), keeping per-function cost minimal despite `Owner`'s large surrounding complexity.\n- `composeAddress` recomputes from current field state on every setter call, so the result is correct regardless of MapStruct's setter-invocation order.\n\nOne design note: the structured lines are `@Transient` (the composed `address` is the persisted column). This satisfies the spec's observable behaviour and all tests, and avoids touching any SQL schema files. If you'd prefer `addressLine1/2` to survive across sessions in the JDBC-backed profiles, say so and I'll promote them to real columns and add the schema entries.",
